## Findings
1. blocking — brief:19 [severity: S1] [design: keeps] The open overlay blanks the page behind it. In the prototype, `.shell--overlay-open .sidebar` is `position: fixed`, which takes it out of the `.shell` grid. `.shell__main` has no grid placement, so it auto-places into the 48px first row and `overflow: hidden` clips it. The page behind therefore disappears rather than being overlaid: shots/inbox-640-overlay-open.png and inbox-390-overlay-open.png are empty right of x=280, while inbox-640-collapsed.png is full. b3's served row checks only `overflow: hidden`, so it passes. Replace with: "…opens it, moves focus to its first link and keeps the main pane in its own row, visible behind the drawer and unable to scroll (`.shell__main { grid-row: -2 / -1 }` below 960)…" [class: tests×ruled-case-uncovered] — check: none: a W-415-b3 served assertion that, while the overlay is open at 640 and 390, `.shell__main`'s box keeps y≈48 and height ≥ 800
2. blocking — brief:23 [severity: S1] [design: keeps] The prototype sets `.seats__confirm-bar { left: var(--sidebar-width) }` and drops the 899px rule. Below 960 nothing resets `--sidebar-width` (it stays 220px; `shell--sidebar-collapsed` applies only when wide), and the ≤599 `left: 0` rule does not reach 600–959. So at 600–959, including the 640 zoom case, the fixed bar starts at x=220 under a top-row shell with no rail. At 640 it is 420px wide, with no wrap, holding target, from, ->, to, bisellium.yml, Confirm and Cancel. Content past the right edge of a fixed bar can't be scrolled to. No row stages a Seats change below 1280. Replace with: "…no screen, document or fixed bar clips beyond the right edge; below 960 `--sidebar-width` is 0 so Seats' confirm bar spans the viewport…" [class: tests×ruled-case-uncovered] — check: none: a W-415-b2 served row at 640 that stages a seat change and expects the bar's x≈0 and its Cancel button's right edge ≤ 640
3. blocking — brief:81 [severity: S1] [design: keeps] The b3 and b4 prototype reds were manufactured from working code (D-024; W-065 05.log). Overlay focus, Escape, scroll lock and storage all land in `feat(W-415): behaviour 1`. Rows b3 and b4 then fail only at a planted `assert.fail("behaviour N is not implemented on this red stage")` (red-b3.out, red-b4.out), and `feat … behaviour 3/4` merely delete that line. All seven served rows land at b1, so the served b3 and b4 rows go green at b1, not "in order" (brief:90). The same applies to brief:83. Replace with: "Red fails by assertion at `opening moves focus into drawer`" (b4: "at `preference uses its ruled key`"). Re-split the prototype so feat b3 and feat b4 carry their own code, and reds-host rejects `assert.fail`. [class: review×evidence-backfilled-or-absent] — check: none: reds-host-r5.sh refuses a red whose only failing assertion is `assert.fail`
4. advisory — brief:23 [severity: S2] The rule that would wrap Board's liveness at 640 is dead. It is scoped to `.shell--sidebar-collapsed`, which App never applies below 960. In shots/board-640-collapsed.png the liveness stays inline while the project explanation compresses to four lines, and no row pins it. Replace with: "At 640 the Board liveness row wraps before its project explanation compresses (the 613–640 rule is unscoped from `.shell--sidebar-collapsed`)", or delete that sentence. [class: spec×promise-unattainable-under-accepted-domain] — check: none: a W-415-b2 served assertion at 640 that `.board__liveness`'s top is below the explanation's top
5. advisory — brief:21 [severity: S2] App's storage effect runs on mount at every width. A narrow mount with an absent or invalid value therefore writes `expanded`, so "Only the >=960px state writes the preference" is not literally true. That is harmless, because expanded is the fallback. Replace with: "Only the >=960px toggle changes the preference; a mount at any width may normalise an absent or invalid value to `expanded`." [class: spec×prose-contradiction-under-revision] — check: none: a b4 served assertion that a 640 first mount with no key leaves `localStorage` empty, or the reworded sentence

